          %h2#in_constructive_mathematics In constructive mathematics
          %p
            In classical mathematics, one could prove that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulated+Cantor+real+numbers"} modulated Cantor real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+Cauchy+complete"} sequentially Cauchy complete
            and equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book+real+numbers"} HoTT book real numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_H
            are still sequentially Cauchy complete, the modulated Cantor real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
            in general cannot be proven to be sequentially Cauchy complete. In particular, this means that the sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
